  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "C. Thankappan vs The Director General Of Police on 2 May, 2012 Author: K.M.Joseph Bench: K.M.Joseph, K.Harilal In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr.Justice K.M.Joseph & The Honourable Mr.Justice K.Harilal Wednesday, The 12Th Day Of December 2012/21St Agrahayana 1934 WP(C).No. 29577 of 2012 (V) --------------------------- Petitioner(S): ------------- C. Thankappan, Aged 65 Years Proprietor, C.T.N.Vegetables Near Government Model High School, Varkala Thiruvananthapuram District. By Adv. Sri.J.Jayakumar Respondent(S): -------------- 1. The Director General Of Police Police Head Quarters, Thiruvananthapuram. 2. The Deputy Superintendent Of Police Attingal, Thiruvananthapuram-695101. 3. The Sub Inspector Of Police Varkala Police Station Thiruvananthapuram District.",
    "facts": "(ii) issue a Writ of Mandamus or any other appropriate Writ, Order or Direction, directing the 10th respondent to take immediate action against the respondents 4 to 9 for their illegal demand of Nokkukooli from the petitioner, in the light of Exhibit P-5 Circular forthwith.\" 2. Briefly put the case of the petitioner is as follows. The petitioner is a senior citizen of India, who is conducting wholesale and retail vegetable business under the name and style of 'C.T.N.Vegetables' near Government Higher Secondary School, Temple Road, Varkala at his 18 Cents of property comprised in Survey No.1011/7 of Varkala Village, after obtaining necessary licenses as evidenced by Exts. P1 to P4. The petitioner employed three permanent workers for loading and unloading works in his shop, since the loading and unloading of the articles in the petitioner's business required special care and caution of the experienced hands. But the respondents 4 to 9 who are the head load workers of INTUC Trade Union of the Ruling party, unnecessarily created problems due to non-payment of 'Nokkukooli' which was banned as per Ext. P5 circular of the 1st respondent. When the petitioner stopped the payment of Nokkukooli, they created unwanted problems and even attacked the petitioner's workers, as evidenced by Ext.P7. Though the petitioner filed various complaints before the police, 10th respondent and also other higher authorities seeking protection of his life and property, nobody had taken any action for redressing his grievance, due to the political interference of the Ruling Party and hence this writ petition.",
